= The rhythm and model mix of learning
The usage views separate when the creator interacted with Keating, which models appeared in the lessons, and what practice was recorded. Weekly activity reveals concentrated periods of exchange; the model wheel shows the configurations behind those exchanges.

#figure(
  image("figures/chart-weekly-activity.svg", width: 100%),
  caption: [Weekly learner, tutor, and tool-result events after fingerprint deduplication. Zero-event weeks remain on the time axis. The observation window spans 9 May–6 September 2026.],
)

*The learning routine was concentrated.* June and July account for 1,023 of 1,248 dated events (82.0%). The weekly peaks show bursts of dialogue and tool use, followed by shorter returns in August and September. The episode findings explain what happened inside those exchanges: reconstruction, challenge, correction, and repair.

#figure(
  image("figures/chart-model-mix.svg", width: 100%),
  caption: [Model mix using distinct assistant events, including intermediate responses. “Other” combines all non-MiniMax recorded labels; the missing-label category remains in the denominator.],
)

*MiniMax shaped the everyday teaching setting.* Its three variants account for 61.3% of assistant events, with M3 and M2.7 contributing similar shares. The remaining 38.7% spans other recorded models and missing labels. This distribution gives the model-diversity account a concrete scale.

#pagebreak()
= Practice, recall ratings, and feedback
The materials and practice views show how Keating supported learning beyond dialogue. Counts of stored materials sit beside the creator's recall ratings, followed by the feedback and question-check records that could inform later teaching.

#figure(
  image("figures/chart-materials-reviews.svg", width: 100%),
  caption: [Horizontal bars compare four material types and four self-rated recall categories. Materials and card-review events use separate count axes. A card can contribute repeated review events.],
)

*The archive contains a varied teaching toolkit and uneven recall experiences.* Plans and verifications are the most frequent stored materials, alongside 21 maps and 10 animations. Of 37 card reviews, 22 (59.5%) were rated Good or Easy; 15 (40.5%) were Again or Hard. The latter group gives the revisiting system a concrete set of difficult recall events to work with.

#figure(
  image("figures/chart-feedback-assessment.svg", width: 100%),
  caption: [Stacked bars show feedback provenance and question-check status within their own denominators. The first bar contains 51 feedback entries; the second contains 13 question checks.],
)

*Most recorded feedback came from Keating's interpretation of the exchange.* Turn analysis supplies 42 of 51 entries (82.4%), compared with eight explicit entries. Question checks provide a smaller channel: five are model-graded and eight remain pending. Completing those checks and eliciting the creator's explanation would give subsequent teaching decisions more direct response evidence.
